## [ISSUE] `isAccessTokenId` matcher never checks the grant type (wrong substring and inverted condition)

**Severity:** HIGH — every integration test that uses `expectCodeToToken` / `expectRefresh` / `expectDeviceCodeToToken` / `expectAuthReqIdToToken` passes whatever grant shortcut ends up in the token ID, so the feature this PR adds has no integration coverage.

### The Problem
```java
String[] items = item.split(":");
if (items.length != 2) return false;
// Grant type shortcut starts at character 4th char and is 2-chars long
if (items[0].substring(3, 5).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
return isUUID().matches(items[1]);
```
There are two bugs on line 483:
1. **Wrong offset.** The encoded prefix is `ss tt gg` (session, token type, grant), so the grant shortcut is `substring(4, 6)`. `substring(3, 5)` returns the last char of the token type plus the first char of the grant. For `onrtac` that is `"ta"`.
2. **Inverted condition.** The code returns `false` when the value equals the expected shortcut, which is the opposite of what a matcher should do.

### Theory / Proof
The encoding is defined in `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.encodeTokenId` as `sessionType(2) + tokenType(2) + grantShort(2) + ':' + raw`, and the decoder uses `encodedContext.substring(4, 6)` for the grant. With the matcher's `substring(3, 5)`:
- `onrtac` -> `"ta"`, `onrtrt` -> `"tr"`, `onrtdg` -> `"td"`, `onrtci` -> `"tc"`, `onltac` -> `"ta"`.

The second character of the token-type shortcut is always `t` (`rt`/`lt`), so the substring always starts with `t`. None of the grant shortcuts `ac`, `rt`, `dg`, `ci` start with `t`, so `equals` is always false. The early `return false` never runs, and the matcher only checks `split(":").length == 2` and that the suffix is a UUID.

### What happens today vs. what will break
Tests pass today, but they would also pass if the grant type were wrong or missing. For example, if `OAuth2GrantTypeBase.createTokenResponse` stopped setting `Constants.GRANT_TYPE`, every token would be encoded with `na`, and `expectCodeToToken` would still pass. The two bugs cancel out. Fixing only one of them would make every test fail, which suggests the matcher was never seen failing.

### Research
Hamcrest's `TypeSafeMatcher.matchesSafely` contract: return `true` when the item satisfies the expectation. Other matchers in this file (`isUUID()`, `defaultRealmId()`) follow that contract.

### Recommendation
```java
@Override
protected boolean matchesSafely(String item) {
    String[] items = item.split(":");
    if (items.length != 2) return false;
    if (items[0].length() != 6) return false;
    // Grant type shortcut is the 5th and 6th characters
    if (!items[0].substring(4, 6).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
    return isUUID().matches(items[1]);
}
```

## [ISSUE] New abstract `getShortcut()` breaks existing custom `OAuth2GrantTypeFactory` providers at startup

**Severity:** MEDIUM — a deployment with a custom grant type built against the previous Keycloak version fails to start after upgrade, not just with that grant disabled.

### The Problem
```java
public interface OAuth2GrantTypeFactory extends ProviderFactory<OAuth2GrantType> {
    String getShortcut();
}
```
`getShortcut()` is a new abstract method. `DefaultTokenContextEncoderProviderFactory.postInit` calls it on **every** registered grant factory:
```java
factory.getProviderFactoriesStream(OAuth2GrantType.class)
        .forEach((factory1) -> {
            OAuth2GrantTypeFactory gtf = (OAuth2GrantTypeFactory) factory1;
            String grantShortcut = gtf.getShortcut();
            grantsByShortcuts.put(grantShortcut, grantName);
```

### Theory / Proof
- JLS 13.5.3 / JVMS 6.5 `invokeinterface`: calling an interface method that the loaded class does not implement throws `java.lang.AbstractMethodError`. That error is not caught here.
- If a factory returns `null` (for example a hand-written stub), `ConcurrentHashMap.put(null, ...)` throws `NullPointerException`.
- An exception from `postInit` propagates out of `DefaultKeycloakSessionFactory.init()` and stops the server.

The grant-type SPI is a documented extension point. `TokenEndpoint` resolves grants by `grant_type` through `session.getProvider(OAuth2GrantType.class, grantType)`, and community extensions (custom token exchange, custom assertion grants) implement it.

### What happens today vs. what will break
All built-in factories are updated in this PR, so the distribution starts. An operator who has a custom grant jar in `providers/` gets `AbstractMethodError` on first boot after upgrading, and Keycloak does not start. Recompiling fails too until they add the method.

### Research
Keycloak usually adds `default` methods when it extends SPI interfaces to keep providers compatible (for example `ProviderFactory.dependsOn()`, `ProviderFactory.getConfigMetadata()`, `EnvironmentDependentProviderFactory`).

### Recommendation
Give it a default, and make the encoder handle a null shortcut:
```java
default String getShortcut() {
    return null; // grants without a shortcut are encoded as unknown
}
```
```java
String grantShortcut = gtf.getShortcut();
if (grantShortcut == null) {
    logger.debugf("Grant type '%s' does not provide shortcut; tokens will be encoded as unknown grant", grantName);
    return;
}
```
In `getShortcutByGrantType`, fall back to `UNKNOWN` when no shortcut is available instead of throwing in `encodeTokenId`.

## [ISSUE] Lazy map refresh bypasses the duplicate-shortcut check and can silently remap an existing shortcut

**Severity:** LOW — this only applies to grants registered after `postInit` (hot-deployed providers), but in that case the failure is silent misclassification instead of an error.

### The Problem
`postInit` detects collisions by comparing map sizes:
```java
if (grantsByShortcuts.size() != grantsToShortcuts.size()) {
    throw new IllegalStateException(...);
}
```
The refresh path added "in case new grant type was deployed" writes both maps without that check:
```java
String shortcut = factory.getShortcut();
grantsByShortcuts.put(shortcut, grantType);   // line 113 - overwrites existing owner of `shortcut`
grantsToShortcuts.put(grantType, shortcut);
```

### Theory / Proof
Suppose a custom grant `urn:example:foo` is deployed later with shortcut `cc`. The first time a token is encoded for it, `grantsByShortcuts["cc"]` is overwritten to `urn:example:foo`. `grantsToShortcuts` still maps `client_credentials -> cc`. After that, every `client_credentials` token (`trrtcc:...`) decodes as `urn:example:foo`, and no error is logged. The two `put` calls also are not atomic with respect to each other, so a concurrent reader can see one map updated without the other. That is benign next to the overwrite, but it is another reason to update the maps through a single guarded method.

### What happens today vs. what will break
With the built-in grants and no hot deployment, the refresh branch never runs, so nothing is wrong today. The branch exists specifically for new grants, and that is exactly the case where the collision check is missing.

### Research
`ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent` returns the existing value, so a collision can be detected atomically.

### Recommendation
```java
String existing = grantsByShortcuts.putIfAbsent(shortcut, grantType);
if (existing != null && !existing.equals(grantType)) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Grant '" + grantType + "' uses shortcut '" + shortcut + "' already used by grant '" + existing + "'");
}
grantsToShortcuts.put(grantType, shortcut);
```
Use the same helper in `postInit` and `getGrantTypeByShortcut`.
